[THOUGHT] Social/occupational history is low-risk: no tobacco, minimal alcohol, right-hand dominance — making functional impairment clinically significant. All eight domains remain incomplete; I have covered 1–6 (chief complaint, course, associated symptoms, PMH, meds/allergies, social/exposures), but not yet domain 7 (targeted physical examination) or domain 8 (diagnostic testing). Before requesting tests, I must perform or request key elements of the physical exam — especially given the focal DIP joint pain after traction injury. Critical exam elements include:  
- Inspection for subtle deformity, skin changes, or nail bed involvement;  
- Palpation for tenderness location (DIP joint vs. tendon insertion vs. distal phalanx);  
- Assessment of active and passive range of motion at DIP and PIP joints;  
- Testing extensor mechanism integrity (e.g., ability to extend DIP against resistance — mallet test);  
- Collateral ligament stability (valgus/varus stress at DIP);  
- Capillary refill and sensation (to rule out neurovascular compromise).  
Since I cannot perform the exam directly, I must request these targeted assessments as part of the clinical evaluation.

[TEST] Please perform and report the following targeted physical examination findings for the right ring finger:  
1. Active extension and flexion at the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joint — specifically, can the patient fully extend the fingertip against gravity?  
2. Passive range of motion at the DIP joint — is it full and pain-limited, or restricted?  
3. Tenderness to palpation — precise location (e.g., dorsal DIP, volar DIP, distal phalanx base, nail fold)?  
4. Stability of the DIP joint to valgus and varus stress.  
5. Capillary refill time in the right ring finger tip.  
6. Sensation to light touch over the volar and dorsal aspects of the distal phalanx.

